.TH LZMA 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
lzma, unlzma, lzcat \- decompress .lzma files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unlzma
.RB [ \-cdfkqtv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B lzma
.RB { \-d | \-t }
.RB [ \-cfkqv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B lzcat
.RB [ \-cdfkqtv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unlzma
decompresses each
.I file
in the legacy
.B .lzma
format of LZMA Utils and the LZMA SDK, and replaces it with a file of the
same name without the
.B .lzma
suffix.
.B lzma
is the same, but requires
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t :
it cannot compress, and without either option it prints a usage message
and fails.
.B lzcat
is
.BR "unlzma \-c" :
it writes the decompressed data of each
.IR file ,
in order, to standard output.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
the program reads standard input and writes standard output.
When standard input is a console, it exits with an error unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
When decompressing to files, a
.I file
whose name does not end in
.B .lzma
is reported and skipped.
With
.B \-c
or
.BR \-t ,
any name is accepted.
The output file is created next to
.IR file ,
read-only if
.I file
is, and must not already exist unless
.B \-f
is given.
Its modification time is the current time.
Once the output is complete,
.I file
is deleted, unless
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-k ,
or
.B \-t
is given.
If the data is invalid or the output cannot be written, the partial
output file is deleted,
.I file
is kept, and the next
.I file
is processed.
With
.BR \-c ,
data decoded before the failure may already have been written.
A
.I file
that ends early after its header, or whose dictionary does not fit in
memory, instead ends the program at once, leaving its partial output
file in place.
.PP
A
.B .lzma
file is a 13-byte header, holding the coding properties, the dictionary
size, and the decompressed size, followed by one LZMA stream.
When the header gives no size, the stream ends with an end marker.
Decoding stops at the size or the marker, and any data after it is
ignored, so only the first of several concatenated files is
decompressed.
The format has no checksum, so damage is detected only where it makes
the stream invalid.
An
.B .xz
file is rejected; decompress it with
.BR xz (1).
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-dk ,
and may follow
.I file
operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
All three names accept every option.
.TP
.B \-c
Write the decompressed data to standard output instead of to files, and
keep the input files.
Implied by
.BR lzcat .
.TP
.B \-d
Decompress.
Required by
.B lzma
unless
.B \-t
is given, and otherwise ignored.
.TP
.B \-f
Force: delete an existing output file, even a read-only one, before
writing it, and allow reading compressed data from a console.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the input files.
.TP
.B \-q
Accepted and ignored.
Errors are still reported.
.TP
.B \-t
Test: decompress each
.I file
and discard the output, keeping the input files.
Overrides
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-v
For each
.I file
decompressed to an output file, report on standard error the space
saved, as a percentage of the decompressed size rounded up:
.RS
.PP
.EX
notes.txt.lzma: 53% \- replaced with notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.IP
The line is the same with
.BR \-k ,
although nothing is replaced.
When the compressed file is the larger, the percentage is a meaningless
large number.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The other
.B BB_
variables described in
.BR busybox (1)
apply to this command as to every applet.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the command line are expanded at startup, for callers such
as
.B cmd.exe
that do not expand them.
A busybox program started without this variable, such as the kit's
shell, sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so the commands it runs do not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask applied at startup.
Only the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
has an effect here: a mask with it, such as
.BR 222 ,
creates output files read-only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, and later arguments that begin with
.B \-
are file names.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option,
.B lzma
without
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t ,
or a failure with any input: it could not be read or is a console, its
name lacks the
.B .lzma
suffix, its output file exists, its data is invalid or truncated, memory
ran out, or its output could not be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decompress a file, replacing it with
.IR notes.txt :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unlzma notes.txt.lzma
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decompress a file and keep the original:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unlzma \-k notes.txt.lzma
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read a compressed file without decompressing it to disk:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lzcat notes.txt.lzma | less
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check every
.B .lzma
file in the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lzma \-t *.lzma
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract a compressed tar archive, which
.BR tar (1)
recognizes by its suffix:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar \-xf src.tar.lzma
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the
.BR lzma ,
.BR unlzma ,
and
.B lzcat
of XZ Utils:
.IP \(bu 2
There is no compression, so
.B lzma
requires
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t .
.IP \(bu 2
The only recognized suffix is
.BR .lzma ;
.B .tlz
is not.
.IP \(bu 2
The output file gets the current time, not the input's modification
time.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options except
.BR \-\-help ,
and
.B \-q
has no effect.
.PP
The suffix is matched case-sensitively, so
.I NOTES.LZMA
is rejected although Windows file names ignore case.
A read-only input file is deleted after decompression like any other,
and
.B \-f
replaces a read-only output file.
.PP
A
.B \-
operand after a
.I file
operand does not read standard input, and fails.
.PP
Decompression holds the dictionary in memory, up to the decompressed
size, and a dictionary may be as large as 4 GiB.
A file with a huge dictionary can fail with
.BR "out of memory" ,
particularly in the 32-bit kit, where busybox has 2 GiB of address
space.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR xz (1),
.BR zstd (1)
